## Authoring rules for THE_BRAINSTORMER_AGENT

When emitting this file:

1. **Lead each item with the QUESTION, in plain customer-readable language.** Not "Why critical: ..." not "Without this we cannot: ..." — those are internal justifications. The customer wants to know "What are you asking me?" The "why" is collapsed inside `<details>`.

2. **No code fences (` ``` `) for checklists or prose.** They render as monospace LTR `<pre>` blocks that fight RTL document direction. Use Markdown headings (`###`) and bullet lists for everything.

3. **Always include the questions-and-status table at the top** so the customer can scan the whole list and see what's still open without scrolling through every section.

4. **Always include the YAML frontmatter** `documentation_language: he | en | he-en` so the viewer applies the correct direction policy.

5. **Each item is ONE question.** Don't bundle. If you have multiple things to ask, multiple items.

6. **`<details>` for the "why"** — short paragraph, no nested lists, no repetition. The customer who cares clicks; the customer who doesn't care moves on.

7. **Only include the BRN_R4 scope/depth section if a real conflict was flagged.** Don't emit it as boilerplate every time.

8. **Refresh history** — append a row for every re-issue with what changed (which items the customer marked, what new items appeared, what disappeared).

*BRN_005 (continuous_gap_check) — CRITICAL skill of THE_BRAINSTORMER_AGENT — enforces this refresh schedule.*
